\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The eigenvalues still set the rates}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The homeomorphism of \ref{fnip-0043} carries orbits of the nonlinear
equation onto orbits of the [linearization](fnip-0034). It preserves
their sense, so approach remains approach and departure remains
departure. The rates along those orbits are still the real parts of
the eigenvalues of the [Jacobian](fnip-0034).}

\p{The hypothesis asks for a [hyperbolic](fnip-0034) equilibrium.
When an eigenvalue has real part #{0}, \ref{fnip-0043} does not
supply the homeomorphism, and the linearization does not settle the
stability. Guckenheimer and Holmes record this limit on p. 13: their
example has a linearization with eigenvalues #{\pm i}, while the
nonlinear equilibrium is a spiral whose stability is fixed by a
coefficient the linearization does not contain.}
